## Defining Total Ordering

```xiom
pub interface Ord: Eq {
  fn cmp(self: &Self, other: &Self) -> Ordering;
  // Ordering is: Less, Equal, or Greater
}
```

### Implementing Ord

```xiom
pub type Person = { name: Str; age: Int; }

impl PartialEq for Person {
  fn eq(self: &Person, other: &Person) -> Bool {
    return self.age == other.age && self.name == other.name;
  }
}
impl Eq for Person {}

impl Ord for Person {
  fn cmp(self: &Person, other: &Person) -> Ordering {
    // Compare by age first, then name
    match self.age.cmp(&other.age) {
      Ordering::Equal => self.name.cmp(&other.name),
      other => other,
    }
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let a = Person{ name: "Alice", age: 30 };
  let b = Person{ name: "Bob", age: 25 };
  if a > b { return 1; }  // true: Alice is older
  return 0;
}
```

### Derived Comparisons

When you implement Ord, you get `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` for free from `cmp`:

```xiom
// These all work once Ord is implemented:
let younger = b < a;    // calls a.cmp(&b)
let oldest = max(a, b);  // Uses Ord
```

### Sorting with Ord

```xiom
fn sort_people(people: &mut Vec[Person]) {
  people.sort();  // Requires Person: Ord
}
```

### Reverse Order

```xiom
fn reverse_cmp[T: Ord](a: &T, b: &T) -> Ordering {
  return b.cmp(a);  // Flip the order
}
```

